## Tutorials

When you know exactly how many times you want to loop through a block of 
code, use theforloop instead of awhileloop:

Statement 1is executed (one time) before the execution of the code block.

Statement 2defines the condition for executing the code block.

Statement 3is executed (every time) after the code block has been executed.

The example below will print the numbers 0 to 4:

This example prints even values between 0 and 10:

This example calculates the sum of numbers from 1 to 5:

This example prints a countdown from 5 to 1:

Just like awhileloop, aforloop may also never run.  
If the condition isfalseright from the start, the code inside the loop will be skipped entirely:

In this example, the loop starts withi = 10.  
The conditioni < 5is already false, so the loop body is skipped, and nothing is printed.

### Code Examples

```
Syntax
for (
statement 1
;
statement 2
;
statement 3
) {
// code block to be executed
}

---

Example
for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
System.out.println(i);
}
